"Card handling" = "Card handling";
"Hold the deck like you mean it." = "How to hold the deck";
"Start with a relaxed grip" = "Keep your grip light";
"Hold the deck lightly: thumb along one long side, fingers along the other. A relaxed hand moves naturally. A squeezed hand looks as if it is hiding something." = "Rest your thumb along one long edge and your fingers along the other. Hold the deck lightly so your hand can move naturally. A tight grip can draw attention to your hand.";
"Square up between actions" = "Square the deck between moves";
"Square the cards before each new action. Neat edges make the next move easier and look tidy from every angle." = "Line up the edges before the next move. A squared deck is easier to handle and looks tidy from the side.";
"Clean first, fast later" = "Practice slowly";
"Learn each move slowly until it is clean every time, then build speed gradually. Speed hides very little. Smoothness and a natural rhythm do far more." = "Repeat the move slowly until you can do it cleanly each time. Pick up the pace a little at a time and keep the same smooth rhythm.";
"Film your hands from a spectator’s eye line. Does your grip look the same before and after the move?" = "Film your hands from the audience’s point of view. Compare how you hold the deck before and after the move.";
"Misdirection" = "Misdirection";
"Guide attention, don’t fight it." = "How to guide the audience’s attention";
"Where you look, they look" = "Look where you want them to look";
"Audiences tend to follow a performer’s gaze. Look at the hand or object you want them to watch, and give it a reason to be interesting." = "People often follow your gaze. Look at the hand or object you want them to notice, and give them a reason to pay attention to it.";
"Hide inside natural actions" = "Give each movement a purpose";
"An action draws less curiosity when it already has a reason, such as putting a pen away or gesturing to a spectator. Movements with no reason invite people to wonder why." = "Putting away a pen or gesturing to someone already has a purpose. A movement like that draws less attention than an unexplained reach.";
"Use the relaxed moments" = "Notice when attention eases";
"Attention often dips after a surprise, a laugh or the end of a sequence. Performers plan their timing around these moments rather than when everyone is watching closely." = "People often relax after a surprise, a laugh or the end of a sequence. Take those moments into account when you plan the timing of a routine.";
"Ask a friend where they were looking at each moment. Their answers are your real script." = "Perform for a friend and ask what they were looking at during each part. Use their answer to adjust where you direct attention.";
"Patter and script" = "Performance script";
"Write the words, then make them yours." = "What to say during a trick";
"Give the trick a reason" = "Start with a simple premise";
"Patter is what you say while you perform. A simple premise, such as why this is happening and why it matters, turns a puzzle into a moment people remember." = "Patter is what you say while you perform. Give people a simple reason for what you’re doing and why they should pay attention.";
"Rehearse out loud" = "Rehearse out loud";
"Say the script aloud while doing the moves. Words and hands compete for your attention, and practising them together shows where you rush or freeze." = "Say your lines as you do the moves. You’ll hear where you rush and notice where your hands stop while you speak.";
"Leave room for the reveal" = "Pause after the reveal";
"Pause when the magic happens. Let the audience react before you speak again." = "Give the audience a moment to react to the effect before you speak again.";
"Record a run-through with sound. Cut any line that does not help the moment land." = "Record a full run with sound. Listen back and cut any line that gets in the way of the reveal.";
"Mirror and camera" = "Mirror and camera";
"Practise for the camera. Perform for people." = "How to review a rehearsal";
"Mirrors reverse the picture" = "Start with a mirror";
"A mirror shows a reversed image and makes you watch yourself while you perform, which can change how you move. Use it for quick checks, not as your only judge." = "Use a mirror for a quick check. The reflection reverses the image, and watching yourself can change how you move. Film a run to see what the audience would see.";
"Check every angle" = "Check the audience’s angles";
"Place the camera where spectators will be: seated, standing, to your left and to your right. Some moves only work from certain angles. Learn them before you perform." = "Put the camera at a seated spectator’s eye level, then at standing height. Check the view from either side too. Find the angles that give away a move before you perform.";
"Review at half speed" = "Review at half speed";
"Play your footage at 0.5× to catch flashes and hesitations. Reframe the clip for a closer look at your hands." = "Play your footage at 0.5× to catch flashes and hesitations. Reframe the clip for a closer look at your hands.";
"One clean run on camera tells you more than ten runs nobody saw." = "Film one full run from where a spectator would sit. Note one spot to practice again.";
"Magic etiquette" = "Magic etiquette";
"Protect the wonder." = "Working with spectators and creators";
"Keep methods among magicians" = "Keep the method private";
"Major magic societies ask members not to expose methods to the public. Keeping secrets protects the experience for audiences and the work of other performers." = "Magic societies ask members to keep methods private. That preserves the surprise for audiences and respects the work of other performers.";
"Credit the creators" = "Credit the creator";
"Many routines are someone else’s creation. Learn where your material comes from, credit its creators, and buy published routines rather than copying them." = "Find out who created a routine and credit them when you use it. Buy the published version when it’s available.";
"Look after your spectators" = "Ask before involving someone";
"Ask before you borrow an item or invite someone to help. Return everything as you found it, and never make a volunteer the target of a joke." = "Ask before borrowing anything or inviting a spectator to help. Return borrowed items as you received them. Keep volunteers out of the punch line.";
"A common rule of thumb: don’t repeat the same trick for the same audience." = "If someone asks to see the trick again, have a different one ready for that audience.";
